 Since $\angle Q=\angle R$, we have $PQ=QR$. Thus, if we let $Q XR=h$, our triangle now looks like: [asy]
pair P,Q,R;
P = (0,0);
Q = (-6,0);
R = (0,-6);
draw(P--Q--R--P);
draw(rightanglemark(R,Q,P,18));

label("$P$",P,SW);

label("$Q$",Q,S);
label("$R$",R,N);

label("$h$",(R+Q)/2,NE);
label("$h$",(R+P)/2,W);
label("$\sqrt{2}h$",(Q+P)/2,W);
draw(rotate(22.5)*(1,-7),dashed);
[/asy] It follows from Pythagoras that $PR = \sqrt{2}h$. Since we are given that $PR=\sqrt{2}h$, this means $h$ is the desired altitude of $\triangle PQR$: $\triangle PQR$ is isosceles and the altitude is the same as one of its sides. We also know that $\operatorname{area}= \frac{bh}{2}$, so this means that $\operatorname{area}= \frac{\sqrt{2}h \cdot h}{2} \Longrightarrow \frac{h^2 \sqrt{2}}{2}$. We are given that $\sqrt{2}h=6\Rightarrow h=3\sqrt{2}$, and substituting this result into our area gives $\boxed{9}$. </think> <answer> 9 </answer>

